    "narrative": "The path to a competitive day for Ghost Ride the WHIP hinges on our middle infielders today, as **Brice Turang** and **Isaac Paredes** anchor the lineup with the top efficiency scores at 63.14 and 66.57, respectively. **Brice Turang** benefits significantly from the leadoff spot against a middle-tier arm in Kirby, providing the high-floor speed production we prioritize in this 5x5 format. Meanwhile, **Isaac Paredes** enjoys a massive 16% lift from the matchup against Grayson Rodriguez; despite the lineup status being officially pending, his expected #3 spot makes him our most potent engine for RBI and HR production. **Max Muncy** rounds out our high-efficiency tier at 62.02, capitalizing on a favorable 4.70 xERA outing against Dobnak, allowing him to serve as a reliable source of power.\n\nThe decision to bench **Vinnie Pasquantino** (48.79) was a marginal call, but ultimately, his struggles against left-handed pitching (15% platoon penalty) necessitated a slide to the pine in favor of players with more favorable daily matchups. **Jose Caballero** remains in the starting outfield despite a significant 13% platoon drag, as his elite stolen base potential remains the primary driver of our roster construction strategy; he is a volatility play, but one that is essential for maintaining our lead in the scarcest category. We are also monitoring the status of **Josh Naylor**, **Travis Bazzana**, and **Austin Riley**, all of whom are hovering in the mid-50s; they are steady but lack the upside of our top-tier starters today.\n\nWe are strictly avoiding any exposure to **James Wood** and **Mickey Moniak** due to their inactive status on the IL and scheduled off-days, respectively. While we continue to look for ways to integrate depth pieces like **Cole Young**\u2014who barely missed the cut\u2014or prospects like **Dalton Rushing** and **Edwin Arroyo**, the current configuration provides the highest probability of hitting our daily efficiency targets. We remain cautious regarding **Jose Caballero**'s late-order placement, as an official lineup shift could further depress his score if he drops lower than expected or faces early-inning pinch-hit pressure.",
